A||||4||Minong||More Lowering Questions||||5||
Z||000000||Minong||03-13-2000||10:19 PM||||<BR>I was thinking about using Koni adjustable shocks with the Fiero Store 1" springs in the front of my 88GT. <BR> <BR>Is anyone using this setup?  <BR>Does everything fit together/travel OK?<BR>Do I have to be concerned about alignment/tire wear?<P>Any other info welcome.||||reg||5
Z||000001||1FST2M6||03-13-2000||10:37 PM||||Not on an 88. i've got a full set of Konis and Eibachs on my 86 and haven't had any problems with anything! running -.5* camber in front, -1* camber on the rear. handles GREAT. But before you pay a fortune from the Fiero Store you might want to get in touch with <A HREF="http://www.shox.com" TARGET=_blank>www.shox.com</A>  a full set of springs, shocks, struts was $618. ||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||03-14-2000||03:04 AM||||The KONI kit from the Fiero Store is $1000 ($900 with fiero club discount) and includes front and rear heavy duty sway bars.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||joeformula88||03-15-2000||09:43 AM||||Minong,<BR>I'm thinking of doing the exact same thing on my Fiero, so I'm very interested in the answers to your questions. I only know that IRM (International Research Motorsports, in Maryland, run by Jim Sutter)had been working on a setup for the '88, but never started marketing this because of the small number of '88's on the market. I was pleasantly surprised to find that since recently the Fiero Store offers the lowering springs, but know of noone who has tried them (yet)..||||reg||1
Z||000004||Minong||03-15-2000||09:59 PM||||FYI<P>Cost for Konis from <A HREF="http://www.shox.com:" TARGET=_blank>www.shox.com:</A> <P>$89.95 each for front<BR>$459 for all 4 corners<P>They also have the springs, but you have to buy a set of 4.||||reg||1
